 "audio_url": null,
 "note_en": "的, 地 and 得 sound the same (de) but do different jobs. 的 comes before a noun: 我的作业 \"my homework.\" 地 comes before a verb and turns a word into an adverb: 难过地坐在衣柜里 \"sitting sadly in the wardrobe.\" 得 comes after a verb and introduces an evaluation: 吵得很大声 \"you're arguing very loudly.\" Chinese schoolchildren mix them up as often as English speakers mix up \"its\" and \"it's.\"",
